.TH CGCHESS 6 "November 2026" "cggames 1.4.0"
.SH NAME
cgchess \- chess against GNU Chess or Stockfish
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cgchess
.RI [ "toolkit options" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cgchess
plays chess against the computer: a board drawn to fit the window, with
GNU Chess, the
.B gnuchess
program, or Stockfish, the
.B stockfish
program, choosing the computer's moves.
.PP
Click a piece of yours: its square lights up, with a dot on each square
it can go to.
Click one of those to move there, or drag the piece to it.
A pawn reaching the last rank asks which piece it becomes.
The last move is shown lit, and a king in check is marked red.
.PP
The rules are kept by
.B cgchess
itself, not by the engine: castling, en passant and promotion, and the
end of the game at checkmate or stalemate, or a draw by the fifty-move
rule, by three repetitions of a position, or when neither side can
mate.
.PP
The board's edge is marked with the files, a to h, and the ranks, 1 to
8.
The moves played are listed beside the board, in algebraic notation,
a line to each move of White's and Black's.
.PP
With a clock, each side has the same time for the whole game.
Each player's time left is shown at the foot of the window, the one
waiting shown grey; the clock starts at the first move.
A player whose time runs out loses.
The computer spends its time to suit what is left.
.SH MENUS
.TP
.B New Game
Starts again, with you playing the side Options says.
.TP
.B Open Game...
Reads a game saved in Portable Game Notation, PGN, and goes on from
its last position.
Comments and variations are skipped, and a game in a file of several
is the first.
.TP
.B Save Game...
Writes the game so far as PGN, which other chess programs can read.
.TP
.B Undo Move
Takes back your last move, and the computer's after it.
With a clock, the clocks go back to what they showed then, so even a
game lost on time can be taken back.
.TP
.B Hint
Asks the engine for a move for you; it is shown lit in blue.
.TP
.B Options...
The options below.
.TP
.B Coordinates
In the View menu: whether the board's edge is marked with files and
ranks.
.TP
.B Move List
In the View menu: whether the list of moves is shown.
.TP
.B Flip Board
In the View menu: turns the board round.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B You play
White or Black; a game under way keeps the side it began with.
.TP
.B Pieces
The Merida pieces, the first choice, or Burnett, Colin Burnett's.
.TP
.B Board
The colours of the board: Wood, the first choice, Green, Blue, Grey,
or XBoard's own yellow and green.
.TP
.B The computer thinks
To a depth, of 1 to 40 half-moves, 2 at first, or for 1 to 120
seconds a move; and at a skill of 0 to 100, 15 at first, for an engine
that can play weaker.
The skill is greyed for GNU Chess, which can't play weaker; another
engine of your own choosing uses it if it can.
Each engine makes its own of a depth: Stockfish looking 2 half-moves
ahead plays far better than GNU Chess does.
With a clock, the engine is told both sides' time left, as in any
timed game, and spends its own as it judges; a depth still bounds its
search, but the seconds a move are for games without a clock.
.TP
.B Clock
None, the first choice, or 5, 10, 15 or 30 minutes a side.
It is used from the next game, or at once if no move has been made.
.TP
.B Engine
GNU Chess or Stockfish, each shown grey if it isn't installed, or
another UCI engine, by the command that starts it; the engine is
started again when it is changed.
.SH THE ENGINE
The engine is a separate program, spoken to in the Universal Chess
Interface, UCI, the whole game being sent with each question.
It is
.B gnuchess --uci
by default, or
.B stockfish
when GNU Chess isn't installed, from the path or from
.IR /usr/games ;
Stockfish, or any UCI engine with its arguments, can be chosen in
Options instead, or named as
.B engine
in the options file below.
.PP
The depth or time is sent as UCI asks it, so any engine keeps to it.
Playing weaker is left to each engine by UCI: the skill is set where
the engine offers a way, Stockfish's Skill Level, or the UCI_LimitStrength
and UCI_Elo options that UCI names but doesn't require.
GNU Chess offers neither, and plays as well as it can, as Options
says.
Hints are asked for a second's thought at full skill.
The About dialog names the engine playing.
It is run in the temporary directory.
Without an engine, you play both sides.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/cggames/cgchess.conf
The options, the engine, and the window's size, or
.I ~/.config/cggames/cgchess.conf
without the variable.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/cggames/cgchess/pieces
The pieces' pictures, a set to each SVG:
.I merida.svg
and
.IR cburnett.svg .
.SH AUTHORS
cgchess was written for CGTK.
The pieces are Colin M. L. Burnett's, and Armando Hernandez
Marroquin's Merida pieces, both under the GNU General Public License,
version 2 or later, as lichess.org has them.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gnuchess (6),
.BR stockfish (6),
.BR xboard (6)
